=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
There is no fault in the network. Direct inspection of every component between laptop2 and laptop3 shows the infrastructure is fully healthy.

Both laptops connect to the same physical switch (switch1): laptop2 arrives on eth3 and laptop3 on eth4. Switch1 places both of those ports into the same Linux bridge, `br_vlan10`, with no VLAN separation between them. Every interface involved is administratively UP and operationally UP with no RX/TX errors. Neither endpoint has any iptables rules installed — all chains are empty with an ACCEPT default policy — so there is no host-level filtering that could block traffic. Both hosts share the 172.16.10.0/24 subnet and each has a consistent kernel route for that prefix on eth0, confirming L3 is also coherent. A live ICMP probe from laptop2 to laptop3 (172.16.10.13) completed 4 of 4 packets successfully with sub-millisecond round-trip times and zero packet loss.

Root cause: No fault exists. The symptom reported by the user — that laptop3 is unreachable from laptop2 — is not reproducible and is not supported by any evidence in the live lab. The connectivity between the two machines is intact at every layer (L1 link state, L2 bridge membership, L3 routing, and L4 host firewall). The helpdesk ticket is based on a false premise; the issue either resolved itself before investigation began or was never a network-layer problem. No corrective action is required on the network side; the user should verify application-layer services (e.g., file-sharing daemon, hostname resolution) on the end hosts themselves.
=== END ===